In article <9qkmo8$1c4$1@kadath.deep.it>, Cthulhu <noone@nowhere.it> 
wrote:

> How much do you think I can offer for an AlphaServer 300, model 4/266,
> 64 MB RAM, 2 x 1GB HDD?
> No monitor, maybe keyboard and mouse (maybe - does it have PS/2 ports?).
> 
> Is it able to run OpenVMS + DECwindow in some working way with only 64
> MB RAM, or is it at least exapndable with common DIMM?

Such a system typically brings 200-400 USD on eBay, though you can 
easily check out what similar systems have gone for recently and not 
take my word for it. Make sure there is a functioning SCSI CD-ROM that 
supports 512k block transfers.  

You can run recent versions of OpenVMS in 64 MB RAM, but just barely.  
Memory required is 72-pin parity RAM (SIMMs not DIMMs), 60 or 70 ns, in 
matching sets of 4.  

There are PS/2 mouse and keyboard ports; most anything will work but 
I'd recommend shopping around for a used LK450 or LK461 for the 
keyboard, and you'll want a 3-button mouse.

The manual for the system is available here:

<http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/archive/300/300ug_ab.html>

The latest firmware is available here:

<http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/readmes/as300.html>

If you want a wee bit more disk space, I just put a Seagate ST15150N 
(4.3 GB) in one of these machines and it works just fine; I got the 
drive at auction for about $25 (note: this particular drive will only 
fit in the upper slot, not the skinnier lower slot).  Most 50-pin 
narrow drives should work, but SCSI being more of a mystical teaching 
than a technology, YMMV.


