I don't know anything about a RQDX3, but if the "hardware format" is the
same as a PC you could use Linux to read the floppy.  If Linux has a driver
for whatever file system
is used on the floppy you could just mount it.  It might just be UFS.
Otherwise you could use dd to copy the floppy to disk. but you would then
need to edit the floppy image.

A month or two ago I downloaded Ultrix 1.0 and 1.2 from the old Unix archive
on the web.   They were compressed so I used gzip to uncompress them.  I
don't recall if I got tar files or a 8" floppy image that I did a loopback
mount on, but in the end I ended up with tar files.  I ran the tar files
through the standard GNU tar that comes with Linux and I got Ultrix out.
All the files for Ultrix where there!  If only I had a VAX 11 system I could
run VAX Ultrix.  I would guess that even my slowest DECStation with MIPS
Ultrix will out run the fastest VAX 11 system with VAX Ultrix. :-)

I used Slackware Linux with kernel version 2.0.35 and I used only the
standard stuff that comes with Linux to do that.

Hope this helps.

Robert



