Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> 
> > Howard S Shubs wrote:
> >>
> > But only a fool would put on computer.  My saying "If its on computer,
> > its not secure."  Nothing, short of keeping it to yourself, is secure -
> > one can merely raise the level of effort required to obtain it.  Even
> > banks are not secure - they simply raise the level of effort required to
> > break-in beyond what most people are willing to exert.

I have a computer security book. It says at the beginning there is no
such thing as computer security. I like this book. It addresses risk and
how to mitigate, there are no cures.
This therefore is very true.
 
> Putting something on a computer is not significantly more risky
> than putting it into a notebook.  Some things are too complex
> to memorize.
> 
> Networking that computer to the outside world, on the other hand,
> introduces a lot of risk.

Again I agree with the first para, but the second, in most cases, does
not necessarily introduce more risk. 

If you have the console, physical access, kiss bye bye to security.
Datacentre systems have an advantage that physical security measures can
be put in place, card keys, security guards, etc. This would not prevent
confident and clever imposters (stolen, dupicated IDs) but only the
systems in place could protect a computer in such a situation.

When you say notebook, what do you mean? The sort that is operated by a
pencil and pages turned by hand, or a portable electronic rodent
controlled computer? (Doesn't matter, if it was intentional your
wordplay was nice.)

Either have the same risk, if you get physical access. Lets say you
wrote it down in some fancy cipher, or used an electronic equivilent.
How long would it take a machine to decode machine data, or break the
written code by hand from those stolen papers? Then again, the
protection offered only has to match or exceed the useful life of the
data.

(If you're using PGP you can make it harder ensuring your private
keyring is nowhere near the encrypted data, but leave a keyring
protected by a simple password/phrase, then you're almost defeating the
power of encryption and reducing it to a simple password)

Lets consider confidential paper file in a filing cabinet, gain physical
access, that is it.

I don't think you can use an argument about knowing what you are doing,
if you consider a theif highly trained to evade physical security, or a
hacker trained in knowing how to get into a system, if that indeed means
getting physical access, then someone is there to get the data.

Heck if someone is determined enough they could do a ram raid on the
side of the datacentre, grab the disk volumes you need and fit them into
a prepared operating system to recover the data. Maybe if it's not
underground, or significantly overground, but determination may breed
ingenuity. 

In summary, preventing people getting their hands on whatever is being
physically protected is probably the most important in security. People
have the threat of life being taken from them in extreme cases, and the
bank above is an example.

Turning to networks, a system using encrypted data, securely locked
away. It is difficult to  prevent 'inside' jobs, you'd hope vetting and
interviewing would save you. From an external point of view if there are
no holes in system security, then what is to stop someone being taken at
gunpoint and forced to reveal a password? 

Alright, a little extreme. Most operating systems employ a good password
encryption system, but TELNET and FTP without kerberos allows plain text
to fly between LAN sniffers. Less complicated maybe stealing something
else that is password protected and discover that password, how many of
us truly use different and unrelated password for all password protected
systems?

And finally, console security, nice little IP capable terminal server
with break enabed console access, perhaps while you're puzzing over an
odd crash dump, someone has opened a door so they can steal your data.
Console on a plate.

But to throw in some hope, stealth plays a large part in protection. A
notebook case is an advertisement, so, don't use a notebook case.

VMS has secondary passwords, the correct use is for two persons to be
present in unlocking access to data, but you're relying on overriding
accounts being secure also.

I'm sorry for being doom and gloomy, but security is only as strong as
the weakest link. Fitting good locks and bolts to a door with weak
hinges may lull you into false security.

I know I'm probably preaching to the converted, but I'd rather you told
me to stop waffling rather than you wake up to discover a security
breach. Nice thread, threw my 0.02 in.

-- 
Regards, Nic Clews CSC Computer Sciences
nclews at csc dot com


