Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> There is no network security nearly so effective as an air gap.

Agreed, but I'd add that it may be no more of a guarantee to data
security, and the network protection could be better than physical
protection.

Here's an example. In a bank that I use occasionally, it was being
refurbished a little while ago, and, I guess to foster good customer
relations during the building work, a nice helpful notice board saying
sorry for the inconvenience, but look at the plans of what it will look
like when it's finished, and a nice copy of the architects work, public
areas and 'secure' areas. This, on public view, would allow potential
weaknesses to be examined and exploited. Useless air gap. (I did speak
with the manager expressing my concerns, and it was removed quite
rapidly).

> Whatever physical security measures you introduce, they are not
> any less effective for a computer than for a notebook in a the
> same physical location.

Spot on Larry, hadn't considered that.

However, cynically, I could wheel a cabinet out and claim me and my
crooked friend are repairing this refrigerator, where a notebook does
not often resemble such a humble appliance. How many security guards
recognise a GS80 at 25 yards?

"Falcon and the snowman" is a fine film, not only for the Pat Metheny
soundtrack, but based on a true story showed how the air gap was
breached. I also like the "Mission Impossible" TV series, 60 minutes of
imaginitive scheming.

Another true story - at a certain place I won't confirm or deny I ever
worked, two youngish gents entered site in their vehicle and parked up.
They were challenged by security, not for their ID's but where they had
parked. They moved the car and carried on in. What air gap?

I guess what I'm trying to say nothing is guaranteed. Air gaps have to
be watertight.

> > When you say notebook, what do you mean?
> 
> A notebook is something made of paper.
> Are you too young to remember notebooks ? :-)

I was once told by my chemistry teacher that a spider with inky legs
could write better than me. My notebook had a number of burn marks and
chemical stains, not entirely innocently obtained. 
-- 
Regards, Nic Clews CSC Computer Sciences
nclews at csc dot com


