Oct. 23, 2001, 4:25PM
Compaq posts $120 million quarterly loss
Reuters News Service

NEW YORK - Compaq Computer Corp. today reported an operating loss for
the third quarter, due to weak technology spending, aggressive pricing,
problems associated with the Sept. 11 air attacks, and a typhoon in
Taiwan, the company said.

Compaq -- the No. 2 manufacturer of personal computers -- also said that
it expects the fourth quarter to be weak. It sees an operating loss of
about 3 cents a share and revenue to be in a range of $7.6 billion to
$7.8 billion.

Analysts had expected Compaq to break even in the fourth-quarter, with
estimates from 19 analysts ranging from a loss of 7 cents a share to
earnings of 9 cents a share, according to Thomson Financial/First Call.

Compaq has ceded market share this quarter to competitor Dell Computer
Corp., which has used aggressive pricing to grow in both the corporate
and consumer markets this year.

For the third quarter, Compaq reported it lost $120 million, or 7 cents
a share, on an operating basis, in line with Wall Street estimates. That
compares to operating earnings of $532 million, or 30 cents a share, in
the year-ago period, the company said.

The Houston-based company said that on a net basis, it lost $499
million, or 29 cents a share in third quarter, compared with earnings of
$557 million, or 31 cents a share in the year-earlier period.

The company's third-quarter 2001 operating results exclude an investment
loss of $514 million, primarily related to Compaq's investment in CMGI
Inc.

After Compaq pre-announced on Oct. 1 that it expected to post a loss in
a range of 5 cents to 7 cents a share, analysts expected a 6 cent per
share loss, according to First Call.

Compaq said that sales fell to $7.5 billion in the quarter, down from
$11.2 billion in the year-ago period.

Earlier this week, market research firm Gartner Dataquest said that
Compaq's market share slipped to 10.4 percent in the third quarter from
13.4 percent in the year-ago quarter. Dell, meanwhile, saw its share
rise to 13.8 percent from 11 percent a year earlier.




