...

But look at income over the last 3 quarters ...

Total income = $429 million,  Enterprise = $ 102 million, Access (ie.
PCs) = loss of $485 million, Global Services = $809 million.

Drop the PCs (please) and we find income $ 914 of which Enterprise was
about 11%.

***
'Enterprise' includes BCSG, ISS, and Enterprise Storage.  Storage was said
to be flat Q-to-Q (which means it likely turned a decent profit, as it has
right along), and the Tandem portion of BCSG was said to be flat with Q3
2000 (which should mean it did quite well indeed).  Alpha revenue seems to
have been down markedly from Q2, but what *was* sold was still probably
reasonably profitable.

So once again we have ISS operating in an 'aggressive pricing environment),
which seems to mean "lost money hand-over-fist in order to try to maintain
market share".  I suspect that with ISS factored out the Enterprise space
would look a lot more attractive (and of course without it a large portion
of the Global Services activity would likely evaporate).
***

Note also that it is very difficult to make a loss with
Global Services, the consulting arm of Compaq.

***
Well, yes:  it's largely head-count, no inventory or R&D costs, and much
more stable than the Q-to-Q sales fluctuations because it's based largely on
*existing* installed hardware (accumulated over the years) rather than new
business.

That's likely why the brain-trust running Q thought that deemphasizing
everything else was such a good idea.  What they failed to take into account
was that without a good continuing sales base it's a great deal harder to
keep the service business, since if customers buy their hardware elsewhere
they'll likely buy their service there as well.

One aspect of the Webcast that I heard before getting bored involved the
fact that the service revenue had skewed away from 'system integration' and
toward customer support and financing.  I suspect if that's true it presages
a down-turn:  when customers start turning elsewhere for real technical
help, their loyalty (beyond the payment period on their existing hardware)
becomes tenuous.
***

By the way, did anyone else notice a total absence of anything about the
transfer of Alpha to Intel ?  What did Compaq do ?  Did they give it
away ???

***
Even Compaq's management was probably too sheepish to list explicitly the
sack magic beans Intel promised them for it.  If any actual discounts
accompanied them, they clearly weren't sufficient to allow PCs or
industry-standard servers to turn a profit.

