In alt.sys.pdp10 Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> wrote:
> Presumably the old DUMPER tapes survive. Just to mark a bit of Arpanet/Internet
> history it would be nice to bring up the SIMTEL20 archives on an emulated PDP-10
> and make it available online. Someone would have to rebuild a KS-10 version of
> TOPS-20 with Internet support though as the currently booting TOPS-20 V4.1 KS-10
> distribution doesn't have Arpanet support built in. Do you still have a
> buildable KS-10 distribution by any chance? Was there ever an official KS-10
> distribution with Arpanet support or was that limited to KL-10 distributions
> only?

Alan:

Yes, I am aware of that because I downloaded some files from SIMTEL20 
system some time ago.  After SIMTEL20 was shut down, all archives were 
moved to somewhere.  I was BITNET user some time ago and e-mailed requests 
to SIMTEL20 to send me files.  When archive-server was shut down, I had 
sent messages to one of BITNET nodes to send me files.  I finally got my 
Internet account (gallux.gallaudet.edu) in rest of my college life.  I was 
able download some files from SIMTEL20 via FTP program.

My development version of TS10 emulator has complete KL10 emulation.  KL10 
TOPS-10 system successfully was loaded and run on my TS10 emulator.  DTE20 
communications are not completely yet because I have to develop PDP-11/40 
emulator to support KL10 emulator cocurrently.

About VAX emulator module, I now am implementing disk/tape drives for MSCP 
controller.  A dot is much closer.  Opps! I mean a dollar because VMS 
always use a dollar on VMS prompt as a dot on TOPS-10 promopt.

I found MSCP information in TOPS-10 v7.03 sources.  Check MSCSER.MAC, 
MSCPAR.MAC, SCASER.MAC, and RAXKON.MAC for more information.  Kevin 
provided me a copy of his code and I learned that some are missing in 
NetBSD files.  TOPS-10 v7.03 sources has more complete information and 
documentation about MSCP communications for HSC50 controller and RA60 and 
RA81 disk drives.  After I studied NetBSD files about MSCP, I clearly 
recongized TOPS-10 files has more complete information.  For example, 
unit flags are not defined in Online End message but MSCPAR.MAC mentions 
unit flags in Online End message.

-- Tim Stark

