Alan Greig wrote:
> Carly tells us she has been talking to analysts and explaining their
> strategy. *If* Carly told the Aberdeen Group that she does not see a
> future for VMS then the Aberdeen Group would not be at all confused in
> suggesting that this be made public as soon as possible once HP takes
> over to get the bad news out of the way.

Capellas also doesn't see a future in VMS, but he was no so stupid as to kill
VMS outright. He knows that if he can sway a certain percentage of VMS
customers to eventually go to NT, then it is worth maintaining VMS on life
support to keep those customers until NT is judged to be good enough. If he
kills VMS outright, he knows that he will lose all VMS customers who will send
all of their business to competitors.

Had Carly been planning this Compaq anihilation long before, it could have
been conceivable to have seen Compaq kill VMS, sending all VMS customers to
competitors, most of which may have gone to HP.

But now that the game has been announced, if either Compaq or HP announce the
death of VMS, they are ensured that customers will go to IBM, SUN or Dell or
even NCR/Futjitsu/others.

So, just because Carly doesn't see a future for VMS doesn't mean that she will
kill VMS. It does mean a status quo for VMS: no marketing, and it will
continue to be ignored.


