>Jeff Campbell wrote:

> >Jerome Fine wrote:
> > This may also be why the RX01/RX02 device drivers were never
> > upgraded for Qbus systems using for example the RXV21 (M8049)
> > module in a V5.0 environment.  However, if I was able to add the
> > extra code to make DYX.SYS capable of handling 22-bit user
> > buffers, then I am certain that the DEC development team prior
> > to 1992 was also able to do so.
> The RX02 driver is another matter as the DEC RXV21 interface card only
> has 18 bit addressing.

Jerome Fine replies:

Just because the hardware on the RXV21 (corrected the name - thank you)
had only 18-bit capability does not mean that the software (obviously with
the extra overhead) was not able to do a double transfer via a bounce buffer
in low memory.  The only real problem was that the first version did the
bounce before the next DMA operation.  This meant that the interleave time
between sectors had to be used for BOTH the DMA transfer AND the
bounce.  With an 11/73 CPU, that was possible.  I found there was
insufficient time to do BOTH on an 11/23 system.  Version 2 was a "bit"
more complicated, but managed to keep up by starting the next DMA as
soon as the previous one was finished (after filling or emptying the silo of
course - which is why the interleave was required in the first place).  The
bounce could then be done at the same time as the next DMA since the data
from the silo was already in the bounce buffer in the case of a read and
inside the silo in the case of a write.  Of course keeping track of both buffers
was the "bit" that was more complicated and a "bit" messy since both were
active at the same time.  However, the transfer rate on the 11/23 was so
slow when every interleave was missed that it was impossible to use
the 22-bit capability otherwise.

I have mentioned this before and I guess that no one uses the RX02
any more or at least not in situations when 22-bit addresses are
required.  Of course, PIP and DUP don't require 22-bit addresses
if they are not run under VBGEXE, so a file transfer is sufficient.

> Of course, the RX01 doesn't do DMA so addressing is not an issue.

I never bothered with the DXX.SYS device driver since I have never
used or acquired an RX01.  So I don't know if DEC changed the code
to allow 22-bit addresses in the V5.0 device driver or if 22-bit addresses
were automatically included if that is where the user buffer was located.
If so, it must have been very frustrating for RX01 users who finally upgraded
to an RX02 after V5.0 arrived.  They were using 22-bit addresses with
the RX01 and the RX02 could not.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine



