sword7@speakeasy.org wrote:
> 
> Hello folks:
> 
> Does anyone have disk geometries about CD-ROM drives (RRD40, RRD45, and
> RRD50 drives)?  I am looking for them because I need them to boot CD on
> my emulator.

The disk geometry is not really useful. Both MSCP and SCSI protocols
treat the disk drives as an array of blocks, from zero to N-1 where N
is the total number of 512 byte blocks available.

An MSCP GET UNIT STATUS command *can* return the drive's geometry. Not
all units (drives) do. For those that do the information is not in a
simple sectors (blocks), tracks (heads), cylinders format.

> 
> I have a copy of RXQD3 disk controller manual and read page C-1 about
> RD51 to RD53 fixed disks and RX50 floppy drive.  I did not understand kind
> of blocks about RBN, DBN, XBN, RCT, etc..  Does anyone have more

RBN: replacement block number, used to replace (substitute) a bad LBN

DBN: diagnostic block number, used for maintenance only

XBN: transfer block number, temporary block used during bad block
     replacement

RCT: replacement and cache table, controls mapping between known bad
LBNs
     and their replacement RBNs

The RQDX3 does controller based bad block replacement. You will not need
to "know" about these block types unless you want to implement the
host based bad block replacement algorithm as well. The KDA50, like
the UDA50, uses host based bad block replacement.

> information about them?  I noticed that total LBNs and user LBNs are
> different execpt floppy drives.  I am figuring out difference between
> total LBNs and user LBNs.  For accessing data blocks, should I use
> total LBN or user LBN as total available blocks to being read and written?

The response packet to an ONLINE MSCP command contains the size of the
drive in user accessible LBNs. Use this number.

The SET UNIT CHARACTERISTICS response packet also contains the
useable LBN size.

