>
>I have a copy of RXQD3 disk controller manual and read page C-1 about
>RD51 to RD53 fixed disks and RX50 floppy drive.  I did not understand kind 
>of blocks about RBN, DBN, XBN, RCT, etc..  Does anyone have more 
>information about them?  I noticed that total LBNs and user LBNs are 
>different execpt floppy drives.  I am figuring out difference between 
>total LBNs and user LBNs.  For accessing data blocks, should I use 
>total LBN or user LBN as total available blocks to being read and written?

The "geometry" doesn't make sense for a CD-ROM (which truly is a
constant linear velocity drive, at least in the RRD40/45/50 systems.)
For all of your purposes, Total LBN's = user LBN's.  On some drives
these number differ because there are spare blocks for block replacement
and spare blocks for configuration information, but these would have
no use for a CD, and no well-written device driver would depend on
the geometry numbers.

Your question, considered academically, is somewhat interesting.
I happen to maintain a database of MSCP device codes (see

http://ftp.ibiblio.org/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/rt/misc/dustat/dustat.mac

) and I would guess that a real KRQ50 puts actual numbers in the
geometry fields, though I don't think VMS cares about them (*maybe*
the error logger would use them if they made sense - I'll look in the
listings and see what they say.)

Note that the KRQ50 returns a different controller ID code (16.) in response
to an inquire than a RQDX3 (19.) or RQDX4 (20.).  Again, no well-written
device driver should care exactly what number is here, but I do realize
that your emulation may be used by some less-well-written OS's!

Tim.



