Jerome Fine replies:

I am not making a criticism of your comment about the V4 device
driver for RT-11 since it is almost 20 years since V5.0 (1983) was
released.  Most likely you might have forgotten that V5.0 of RT-11
was the first version that supported more than 256 KBytes of memory.
Consequently, any V4 device driver would not have bothered about
a 22-bit DMA capability since anything over 18-bits (256 KBytes)
was not part of the operating system in any case.

This may also be why the RX01/RX02 device drivers were never
upgraded for Qbus systems using for example the RXV12 (M8049)
module in a V5.0 environment.  However, if I was able to add the
extra code to make DYX.SYS capable of handling 22-bit user
buffers, then I am certain that the DEC development team prior
to 1992 was also able to do so.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine



