In article <3bbc4cf5$1@news.unibe.ch>, "Stephen Ferguson" <ferguson@NOSPAM.bluewin.ch> writes:
:I have just inherited one of these, as it runs a small computer tomogram
:(CT) machine.  

  Which VAXstation 4000?  There are several models, including the
  VLC, the model 60, and a few variations in the model 9x series.
  How are you connecting the keyboard to the system?

:...The computer boots, goes through the POST with no errors, and gives
:me a login screen.  The mouse works.  The KB doesn't.  Yes, it's plugged in 
:and yes, it worked when it was last used by the previous owner.

  What has happened since it worked?  Did the keyboard get dropped, etc?

:If I halt the system to get a console prompt, the KB still doesn't work.

  It would appear you have a failed connector, a failed cable, a failed
  socket, a failed keyboard, or a problem with the motherboard.  (It is
  rather weird that this gets through the self-test.)  Do you get any
  lights on the keyboard when you power up the system?

:I imagine I
:can't work around this by using a PC as a terminal, because the CT software
:uses the DEC's windows environment for its displays.

  It'll be ugly to work around this, that's for sure.  You could use the
  display, but you'd have to run the commands from the serial console 
  terminal and direct the DECwindows output to the workstation.  (This 
  will work, but your applications may not be coded to expect this, and 
  this configuration might well confuse your operators.  Something I'd
  not want to do with a CT system.)

:Any other clues I should look for?

  Broken wires, bent connectors, etc.

:Can I test the KB itself?

  Swap it to another RJ4-based (old VAXstation) system and try it?

:Can I still buy an LK401 KB in Europe?  

  Used, most certainly.  LK401, LK40W, LK402, LK201, LK421, could 
  probably all serve here.  The keyboard layouts of the LK40x series 
  are all similar (though colors and key labels can differ), the LK201 
  has a few less keys, and the LK421 is the compact LK401 keyboard -- 
  it's "missing" both the editing key cluster and the numeric keypad 
  cluster.

:Or can I patch something together from another KB?

  Probably not.


