A little history here - Acorns originally started as 6502 based machines,
these being in rough order:

Acorn Atom
BBC model B/Acorn Electron
BBC Master

They then went to ARM machines, the first of these (the A300 if memory serves)
was known as the Archimedes, and so on until the last of the line machines w
hich were the A7000+ based on a 45MHz ARM7 and the RiscPC which was StrongARM
based.

The first of these machines, and IIRC the "notebook" the Acorn A4 ran an
odd OS called Arthur from ROM, later running RiscOS, again from ROM.

Some of the ARM based systems (those with an R designation) ran a strange
version of UNIX, but not the aforementioned RISC/OS.

Acorn never made a MIPS based machine, I think the MIPSstation was probably
either an ULTRIX box made by DEC or a licensed clone of the MIPS Magnum
in which case it will probably run RISC/OS from disk.

If it comes up in an ARC console then it is most likely a Magnum clone.

