> > Consider that unless proven otherwise, Tru64 is as good as dead. Once a
> > customer accepts that, he also accepts that full migration to another
platform
> > will be necessary at which point endianness becomes irrelevant.
>
> I think endianness is overrated.
>
> I have been heavily involved in porting a major (millions of LOC)
application
> from 16-bit little endian and 36-bit 1s-complement hardware to 32-bit
> big-endian to 64-bit little-endian... with detours to 32-bit little-endian
> and back to 32-bit big-endian... with some of the development on 16-bit
> big-endian and 16-bit word-oriented machines. Word size is a much bigger
> issue than byte order, if you've paid any attention at all to portability.

And that's the point:  without examining the code, you don't know.  Even if
the code *seems* to run fine, there's no guarantee that you've executed all
its paths.

Endianness is indeed a relatively minor problem when porting.  The issue
here is not the difficulty of the port, but whether the port can in any way
be considered transparent - and unless you define examination of every line
of code (even if you find nothing needs changing) as 'transparent', it
can't.

- bill





