Alan Greig wrote:
> The port is undeniably the right thing to do. It's about two years since I
> posted that VMS must be ported to IA64 or die when Compaq killed Alpha -
> something I always expected after the termination of Alpha/NT. Just didn't
> expect the announcement quite this soon. It's the ending of Alpha that was
> wrong - not the VMS IA64 port.

Well, yes and no.

You can't honestly say that Alpha was stopped dead in its tracks because
we haven't had the EV7 systems, they are still due for delivery with
EV79 in the 2003 2004 timeframe. EV8, and EV9, EV10 has stopped. So
we've effectively got a "stopping distance" and a warning. As I
mentioned before, would anyone have supposed that the introduction of
Alpha would have 'killed' the VAX? Well it happened, and I can tell you
there are people non too happy with that, they expected the VAX to live
on and on, beyond _my_ retirement. Not happening.
So they adopt Alpha and take advantage of the migrationary kludges to
have their "VAX in an Alpha". With what is happening now, these people
have realised, today, that their multi million future investment in VMS,
NOT the hardware, is protected by thinking in a platform independent
way. The CETS presentations give some of the details of how to handle
the Alpha to Itanium transition, in addressing this they also realise
they have to bury the VAX. (And anything that 'locks' you into Alpha).

The most important thing is, they know this _now_, today. OK, it is
extra effort, more work, but this stands them in good stead for the
future. Works on Alpha, will also work on X.

Why do I say X? Well check out Clair Grants presentation from CETS, the
last slide "Portable OpenVMS". I guess it's open and now in the public
domain that this is "more than a port to IA-64". (Disclaimer: I know
there is no commitment, I didn't say it, I guess Clair didn't commit to
it either, but I wasn't at CETS, but it _was_ in his slides. I was in
possession of  "this strong rumour" over which I bit my tongue a while
back.)

Itanium not fast enough for you sir? Well, run it on the Power4.

Take a look at the write ups by Paul DeMone, but with the above in mind,
when Paul was writing about the Power4, and comparing it to EV8, the EV8
would be given a run for its money, and any delivery failures would
probably see Alpha falling back in the performance stakes. Although that
wasn't said, it was implied in the article.

I accept that the Power4 itself may also have a rival, as yet unnamed,
hence the ? for the platform architecture in Clair's slides.

Pain and gain, but lets not forget the confidence building. Just how
exciting do you want this to get? I appreciate you're feeling pain from
this Alan, but I see a lot more reasons to be positive. It really is a
waste of time arguing about the performance of Itanium, because there is
no commitment to _keep_ VMS on IA-64!
(I'm on the verge of getting my wrists slapped posting this, so I'll add
that this is my opinion and industry observation, and 'commitment' was
not discussed).
-- 
Regards, Nic Clews CSC Computer Sciences
