Hello,
   I've been working on a hobbyist VAX 8530 system (got it from my school,
RPI) and all has been going well until very recently.  A few days ago,
I attempted to boot Standalone BACKUP to begin installing VMS on it.
About halfway through, the system halted for no apparent reason.  After
a brief period of confusion, I decided to try the boot again.  So I 
did a
 >>>@sysinit
And off it went, loading its microcode.  However, when the SYSINIT.COM
script got to the point where it wanted to do an INIT CPU, I recieved 
a series of error messages that I hadn't before:

*** ERROR Timed out waiting for CSM status
*** WARNING REVR1 IPR not initialized
*** WARNING REVR2 IPR not initialized
*** WARNING SID IPR not initialized

Hmm, looks like the microcode isn't happy...

So I got really annoyed and brought the system down

>>>POWER OFF

and up.

>>>POWER ON

Now, I got another wierd error

*** WARNING CSEQ found locked, CPU control inhibitted
*** INFO CSEQ unlocked

From what I can tell, it looks like the console<->VAX communication
has been screwed up.  The manuals seem to tell me that CSEQ locks when
the VAX senses that the console got power-cycled.  This, however, is
untrue.  The console was on the whole time!  So I did basically what
I can with this machine: I reseated every board in the VAX and every
apparently related cable.  No avail.

So basically a few questions

1) Where (physically) does the CSM (Console Service Microcode) live?

2) What might cause the VAX to improperly sense a console power-failure?

3) How might I go about fixing this problem?

Thanks,

Michael Robinson
RPI Electronics Club Vaxherd
