JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
> Consider that unless proven otherwise, Tru64 is as good as dead. Once a
> customer accepts that, he also accepts that full migration to another platform
> will be necessary at which point endianness becomes irrelevant.

I think endianness is overrated.

I have been heavily involved in porting a major (millions of LOC) application
from 16-bit little endian and 36-bit 1s-complement hardware to 32-bit
big-endian to 64-bit little-endian... with detours to 32-bit little-endian 
and back to 32-bit big-endian... with some of the development on 16-bit
big-endian and 16-bit word-oriented machines. Word size is a much bigger
issue than byte order, if you've paid any attention at all to portability.

The big issues, really, are API mismatches. Particularly when they've been
written to a standard as badly subject to the telephone effect as some of
the ones I've seen.

